This implementation uses the concept of modular exponentiation with a recursive approach. It handles the base case where $ n = 0 $ and returns 1, as $ 2^0 = 1 $. For even values of $ n $, it recursively computes $ 2^{n/2} \mod p $ and squares the result to get $ 2^n \mod p $. For odd values of $ n $, it computes $ 2^{n-1} \mod p $ and multiplies by 2, taking the modulus at each step to prevent overflow.

The time complexity is $ O(\log n) $ due to the divide-and-conquer nature of the recursion, and the space complexity is also $ O(\log n) $ due to the recursion stack depth. This approach efficiently handles large values of $ n $ without causing integer overflow issues.

